rking file. Because our system is working. What maybe I would fill in that gap? is just the VoiceBridge, right? Because the VoiceBridge is going to become my mini command queue for handoffs and files. So think of it almost as a Windows Explorer window. But I could easily get the handoffs. Coda yourself is in the Brave UI, right? The Brave window HTML. The top Kimi role is also Kimi subscription. What we're going to have to do is tell Talon to— when those terminals open, one needs the command within the Kimi JSON to open K3-256K in low thinking. And for now, as the principal consultant, we're using Kimi 2.8, the new preview. Both need to be in YOLO. "]}, "create_time": 1789707019.966, "end_turn": null, "id": "ff9e48da-a265-4d74-bab2-3f552880cb7b", "metadata": {"can_save": false, "message_source": null, "request_id": "8dad1ac9-96f1-40c5-b305-f736890eb035", "selected_sources": [], "serialization_metadata": {"custom_symbol_offsets": []}, "turn_exchange_id": "36b9ffd0-5015-4536-b3ae-7e1a59a48dc8", "turn_id": "7c473113-3662-40f4-8c55-1d96d84d722b", "working_turn_id": "36b9ffd0-5015-4536-b3ae-7e1a59a48dc8"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": 1789707036.806384, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": "final", "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["Yes. That’s a clean evolution of the 3×3, and it actually makes the board more coherent.\n\nThe revised command center becomes:\n\n| | | |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Coda** — Brave UI / ChatGPT | **Kimi** — Technical Stack Methodology | **Chiave** — Architecture |\n| **Principal Consultant** — Kimi 2.8 Preview | **VoiceBridge / Command Queue** | **Calibro** — Implementation Design |\n| **Talon** — Operations | **Keel** — Build / Test / Evidence | **Luce** — Verification |\n\nThe important architectural change is the middle cell. That is no longer a passive “session dump.” It becomes an **active operating surface**: VoiceBridge as the local handoff/queue/file-access pane. Conceptually, almost a lightweight GCE Explorer + command inbox. That fits much better with where the system has gone.\n\nFor the two Kimi seats, I’d bind them explicitly rather than letting them inherit whatever model the Kimi client last used:\n\n- **Kimi / Technical Stack Methodology** → K3-256K, **low thinking**, YOLO\n- **Principal Consultant** → Kimi 2.8 Preview, YOLO\n\nAnd I’d make that part of the seat registration contract, not something Talon has to remember manually every time. Talon can own launch/orchestration, but the desired runtime belongs in the seat definition.\n\nSo for those two seats the registry eventually wants something like:\n\n`seat = Kimi_Methodology`\n`provider = Moonshot`\n`client = Kimi subscription`\n`model = K3-256K`\n`thinking = low`\n`mode = YOLO`\n\nand:\n\n`seat = Principal_Consultant`\n`provider = Moonshot`\n`client = Kimi subscription`\n`model = Kimi 2.8 Preview`\n`mode = YOLO`\n\nThat gives us a much stronger rule:\n\n> **Talon launches the seat. The seat definition determines what runtime should occupy it.**\n\nThat keeps runtime choice out of ad-hoc launch logic.\n\nAnd for Coda, yes: **Brave browser window / HTML ChatGPT surface** is the correct current embodiment. Again, the browser window is not Coda; it is the current runtime surface for the Coda seat.\n\nThis is enough to start turning the old 3×3 layout into a real **registered command-center topology** instead of nine windows arranged nicely."]}, "create_time": 1789707024.173754, "end_turn": true, "id": "c96d5e44-a269-40fc-ac2f-013985d7141d", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [], "content_references": [], "conversation_followup_suggestions_eligible": true, "cot_version": "v5", "default_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "finish_details": {"stop_tokens": [200002], "type": "stop"}, "is_complete": true, "message_type": "next", "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "model_switcher_deny": [], "parent_id": "e4aecd53-2238-4609-973d-95061d61c7fd"