 Board updated — GCE_DON\\command-center\\config.json, named by stable seat identity, with the runtime/model split stored as data (not labels). All 9 panes valid, renderer.js syntax clean, all 8 identity homes exist.\nCoda — Brave UI / ChatGPT      Corrado — Tech Stack Methodology / Kimi   Chiave — Architecture\nRagno — Principal Consultant / Kimi   VoiceBridge / Command Queue        Calibro — Implementation Design\nTalon — Operations             Keel — Build / Test / Evidence            Luce — Verification\nAlso added: Ragno pane now points at GCE_IDENTITY\\Ragno (was the superseded Principal_Consultant), and a new voice pane type for the center tile (shows VoiceBridge RUNNING/STOPPED + Restart).\nFour things to flag before we go further:\n1. Kimi has no cell. The old board's Kimi CLI tile is gone, replaced by Corrado. But your FYI says everyone but Kimi is on OpenCode — implying Kimi still lives somewhere. Where does the Kimi seat sit now?\n2. \"open Kimi surface\" vs the FYI. Your launcher line said Corrado/Ragno → open Kimi surface; the FYI says OpenCode for everyone but Kimi. I read it as: Corrado/Ragno are OpenCode seats running Kimi models (K3-256K / Kimi 2.8 Preview) via the Kimi subscription. That's what I encoded. Correct me if you meant the Kimi app surface.\n3. The launcher can't force model/thinking/mode yet. handleOpencodeSeat spawns opencode with no args — so K3-256K / low / YOLO is recorded in the board as the registry seed but not consumed. Wiring that is the next build, on your go.\n4. Coda's tile says Brave but the fallback still opens Chrome (CHROME_BIN in server.js). Want me to switch it to Brave?\nAnd the center voice tile shows status + Restart — the live Command Queue still lives in the VoiceBridge window; rendering it in the tile is a small server+Python bridge when you want it.\nIf you meant a different board (not the Command Center), say so and I'll move it."]}, "create_time": 1789707278.731, "end_turn": null, "id": "803e1bb3-f7a9-4699-9932-1792ccd02852", "metadata": {"can_save": false, "message_source": null, "request_id": "df2a9e57-c3bd-483a-8ca7-8617da56de9e", "selected_sources": [], "serialization_metadata": {"custom_symbol_offsets": []}, "turn_exchange_id": "9ba346f0-5c94-4bd6-8897-b617a0fafef6", "turn_id": "15a3af9b-1841-49a0-b0de-436ce49c3cfa", "working_turn_id": "9ba346f0-5c94-4bd6-8897-b617a0fafef6"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": 1789707303.046425, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": "final", "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["Yep. He’s very close; the confusion is exactly the **MODEL != SEAT** boundary. I’d send him this:\n\n> **Straightening the board state — your board is the right board. Do not create another Kimi tile.**\n>\n> **1. There is no separate “Kimi seat” anymore.**  \n> The former Kimi methodology identity is now **Corrado**.  \n> Kimi/Moonshot is the runtime/model provider underneath Corrado, not another organizational seat.\n>\n> So:\n> - **Corrado = Technical Stack Methodology**\n> - **Ragno = Principal Consultant**\n> - both currently use Kimi models\n> - there is no tenth Kimi pane and no missing Kimi cell.\n>\n> **2. Corrado and Ragno are OpenCode terminal/runtime surfaces, not the Kimi desktop/web app.**  \n> My wording “open Kimi surface” was imprecise. What I mean is: launch the OpenCode seat using the correct Kimi runtime configuration.\n>\n> Current bindings:\n>\n> **Corrado**\n> - identity: Corrado\n> - role: Technical Stack Methodology\n> - host/surface: OpenCode\n> - provider/runtime family: Kimi / Moonshot subscription\n> - model: **K3-256K**\n> - thinking: **low**\n> - execution mode: **YOLO**\n>\n> **Ragno**\n> - identity: Ragno\n> - role: Principal Consultant\n> - host/surface: OpenCode\n> - provider/runtime family: Kimi / Moonshot subscription\n> - model: **Kimi 2.8 Preview**\n> - execution mode: **YOLO**\n>\n> So yes: what you encoded — OpenCode seats whose runtime/model configuration points at Kimi — is the correct interpretation.\n>\n> **3. Next build should consume the registry seed.**  \n> Right now the board correctly records the desired model/thinking/mode, but `handleOpencodeSeat` ignores it. That is the next thing to wire.\n>\n> Do **not** hard-code “Corrado means K3” or “Ragno means 2.8” into the launcher. The launcher should read the seat definition from `config.json`/registry:\n>\n> `seat -> host -> provider -> model -> thinking -> execution mode`\n>\n> Then construct/apply whatever OpenCode/Kimi config or launch command is required.\n>\n> That way changing Corrado from K3 to another model later is a data change, not a launcher rewrite.\n>\n> **4. Yes, switch Coda fallback from Chrome to Brave.**  \n> Coda’s current surface is **ChatGPT in Brave**. Brave is embodiment/runtime surface; Coda remains the seat identity.\n>\n> **5. VoiceBridge center tile is correct as implemented for this stage.**  \n> Keep **RUNNING / STOPPED + Restart** now. Do not overbuild the queue UI yet.\n>\n> The eventual center tile is:\n>\n> **VoiceBridge / Command Queue / Handoff Surface**\n>\n> Think lightweight GCE Explorer/inbox: handoffs, queued commands, files, runtime messages. But current status + Restart is enough for the first command-center pass.\n>\n> **6. Ragno path change is correct.**  \n> `GCE_IDENTITY\\Ragno` replaces the superseded `Principal_Consultant` identity home. Preserve the old material as provenance if needed; do not route the live seat through it.\n>\n> **Current 3×3 remains:**\n>\n> `Coda | Corrado | Chiave`  \n> `Ragno | VoiceBridge | Calibro`  \n> `Talon | Keel | Luce`\n>\n> **Build order from here:**  \n> Registry/seat definition → OpenCode launch consumes runtime settings → verify Corrado → verify Ragno → Brave correction → stop and test.\n>\n> Don does **not** want additional command-center features layered in during this pass.\n\nThe key correction is that **Kimi did not disappear; it moved down one architectural layer**. It is no longer what we name the box. Corrado and Ragno are the boxes; Kimi is currently what occupies them.\n\nAnd this is actually our first real proof of the registry doctrine from earlier tonight: **seat identity remains stable while runtime configuration is data.** memcite"]}, "create_time": 1789707282.917957, "end_turn": true, "id": "b7c2b48f-7224-45cd-91a0-515d3edadd8b", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [], "content_references": [{"alt": null, "end_idx": 3616, "invalid": false, "matched_text": "memcite", "prefix": null, "prompt_text": null, "refs": [], "safe_urls": [], "start_idx": 3607, "type": "hidden"}], "conversation_followup_suggestions_eligible": true, "cot_version": "v5", "default_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "finish_details": {"stop_tokens": [200002], "type": "stop"}, "is_complete": true, "message_type": "next", "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "model_switcher_deny": [], "parent_id": "97615442-04e9-42ac-aee9-376a97806ec1", "request_id": "df2a9e57-c3bd-483a-8ca7-8617da56de9e", "resolved_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "search_result_groups": [], "story_events": [], "thinking_effort": "standard", "turn_exchange_id": "9ba346f0-5c94-4bd6-8897-b617a0fafef6", "turn_id": "e645c2a9-28f3-4f12-bda6-34adac3bbb98", "working_turn_id": "9ba346f0-5c94-4bd6-8897-b617a0fafef6"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": 1789707312.126334, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "system"}, "channel": null, "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": [""]}, "create_time": 1789707284.1842916, "end_turn": null, "id": "897b2149-aed1-4158-8e23-5a5bece58dbd", "metadata": {"can_save": false, "chime_version": 1, "classifier_response": "default", "cot_version": "v5", "default_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "is_visually_hidden_from_conversation": true, "message_type": "next", "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "parent_id": "bb10429f-8bd8-4924-b351-4a0f90966427", "reasoning_start