a known range or to expand relevant results. Full file size: 198 file lines.", "[L1] # Mobile Executor Layer — Architecture\n\n[L3] ## Design spine (the three rules that keep this bounded)\n\n[L5] The failure mode for this layer is scope creep into \"a second AI runtime with a screen.\" Three rules prevent it:\n\n[L7] 1. **Plans are data, not programs.** The phone executes a closed, versioned vocabulary of declarative operations. No plan-side scripting, no eval, no undeclared loops. The only \"logic\" a plan may carry: declarative preconditions, declared compensations, and output references wiring step results into later params. All control flow lives in one small, versioned interpreter.\n[L8] 2. **Nothing on the phone originates mutation.** The phone's verbs are: capture, forward, approve/refuse, execute-declared-steps, halt, evidence. Any free-text or voice input on-device is *capture*, never a command line. Contractually ban the rot vectors: on-device fallback planner, a chat box that \"helpfully\" executes, smart retries.\n[L9] 3. **Evidence is the write path.** Canonical state = fold(evidence events). There is no second \"state update\" channel to drift out of agreement with the evidence log.\n\n[L11] (On-device ASR is fine — transcription is capture, not decision. No planning models on-device, ever.)\n\n[L13] **Pushback on the brief before I extend it:**\n\n[L15] - *\"Every execution returns evidence and updates canonical state\"* — invert it. If execution both writes state and emits evidence, you have dual writes and eventual disagreement. Evidence → fold → state. One channel.\n[L16] - *Determinism:* the interpreter can be deterministic; the external world cannot. Spend the determinism budget on the interpreter and make **read-back verification** load-bearing: a 200 OK is hearsay; \"succeeded\" means *verified in the target system*.\n[L17] - *Rollback is mostly fiction* for the classes that matter (send, pay, hard delete). Design for declared compensations and honest halts — the UI must be able to say \"email was sent; steps 3–4 did not run\" — not transactional rollback.\n[L18] - *Throughput is capped by human approval, by design.* Pre-authorization is the pressure valve **and** the largest long-term risk surface. Manage pre-auth scopes like production credentials.\n\n---\n\n[L22] ## 1. Component architecture\n\n```\n[L25] ON-DEVICE (bounded control plane)                       REMOTE (GCE / Aegis)\n[L26] ┌─────────────────────────────────────────┐   events    ┌────────────────────────────┐\n[L27] │ Capture      (mic · screen · sensors)   │ ─ outbox ─▶ │ Projection / Committee     │\n[L28] │ Normalizer   (fixed schema, no LLM)     │             │ (all models live here;     │\n[L29] │                                         │ ◀─ plans ── │  propose-only, sign plans) │\n[L30] │ Plan Receiver (sig·schema·risk check)   │             └─────────────┬──────────────┘\n[L31] │ Command Queue (persistent, journaled)   │                           │\n[L32] │ Approval UI   (biometric gate, diffs)   │             ┌─────────────▼──────────────┐\n[L33] │ Executor Core (deterministic interpreter)│  evidence  │ Canonical State (fold of   │\n[L34] │  ├ Adapters: calendar · tasks · email   │ ─ append ─▶│ evidence) · Authority /    │\n[L35] │  │ files · notifications · device       │            │ Policy svc · Audit         │\n[L36] │  └ Evidence Ledger (append-only)         │ ◀ deltas ─ │                            │\n[L37] └─────────────────────────────────────────┘            └────────────────────────────┘\n[L38]      Executor talks to external systems directly via adapters.\n[L39]      GCE connectivity is NOT required during execution — plans are\n[L40]      self-contained signed data.\n```\n\n[L43] - **Capture → Normalizer:** raw artifacts to typed event envelopes. Fixed mapping, deterministic, versioned. No interpretation.\n[L44] - **Plan Receiver:** validates proposer signature, schema version, risk tier, delegation scope. Unknown op → reject at the door.\n[L45] - **Command Queue:** persistent local store + step journal; size-capped (e.g., 100 plans; prune unapproved oldest, never auto-prune approved).\n[L46] - **Executor Core:** interprets plans step-by-step against adapters. No GCE calls during execution.\n[L47] - **UI surfaces — exactly four:** Queue, Approvals, Exceptions, Evidence. Any text input routes to Capture, never around the pipeline.\n[L48] - **Sync Agent:** uplink evidence (append-only, idempotent by event ID), downlink state deltas.\n\n[L50] ## 2. Trust and authority boundaries\n\n[L52] | Concept | What it is | Where it lives | Must never be |\n[L53] |---|---|---|---|\n[L54] | **Identity** | Stable principal (user, device, service); hardware-backed device keypair | Device keystore + authority service | Inferred from voice/content |\n[L55] | **Role** | Session-scoped function: capturer, approver, executor, adapter | Session context | Conflated — approver requires a biometric-verified session; executor can never self-grant |\n[L56] | **Authority** | Signed, scoped, expiring permission: delegation, pre-auth rules, approval tokens | Policy artifacts, verifiable offline | Stored in model weights or app logic |\n[L57] | **Model** | Proposer producing signed plans | Remote service | Executor or authority source; swapping models changes zero trust semantics |\n\n[L59] Boundaries, each with a distrust assumption:\n\n[L61] - **Capture → system:** capture output is untrusted *content*. No code path from mic/screen to any adapter — structurally impossible in this architecture.\n[L62] - **GCE → phone:** plans must be signed and schema-valid; the phone distrusts a compromised GCE.\n[L63] - **Human → system:** approval tokens (device-key-signed, biometric-session-bound, TTL'd) are the only source of L2+ authority.\n[L64] - **Phone → external systems:** least-privilege OAuth per adapter; tokens in keystore, never in the queue or evidence.\n[L65] - **Phone → GCE:** evidence is append-only; GCE periodically read-back-verifies a sample against external systems. GCE distrusts a compromised phone.\n[L66] - **Policy issuance → enforcement:** signed policies cached locally, verifiable offline, refreshed with revocation checks.\n\n[L68] Dual enforcement throughout: local policy check *and* remote audit verify each other's signed artifacts.\n\n[L70] ## 3. Executor state machine\n\n[L72] **Plan-level states:** `proposed → approved → running → terminal ∈ {succeeded, failed, rolled_back, partial_halted, stale, expired, superseded, rejected}`\n\n[L74] **Step-level states (inside running):** `pending → checking → executing → {confirmed | absent | unknown | failed}`\n\n[L76] | From | Event | To | Guard |\n[L77] |---|---|---|---|\n[L78] | proposed | approval token / pre-auth match | approved | token valid, plan unexpired, risk tier matches |\n[L79] | proposed | user deny / TTL pass | rejected / expired | |\n[L80] | approved | start | running | preconditions pass, entity locks acquired, `base_state.state_version` current |\n[L81] | approved | precondition fail | stale | auto-returned upstream for re-projection |\n[L82] | running | all steps confirmed | succeeded | every step `verify()==confirmed` |\n[L83] | running | step failed, compensatable | rolled_back | declared compensations executed in reverse and confirmed |\n[L84] | running | step failed after an irreversible step executed | partial_halted | human exception raised — never auto-improvise compensation |\n[L85] | running | unknown outcome | (verify first) | retry iff `verify()==absent` and step idempotent; irreversible+unknown → exception |\n[L86] | running | newer plan, same entity scope, no irreversible step executed | superseded | |\n[L87] | any | crash/reboot | resume from journal | verify-first every in-flight step before proceeding |\n\n[L89] **Determinism, operationally:** given (plan, precondition results, step results), the sequence of queue transitions and evidence records is a pure function. No wall-clock in branching decisions (time enters only as TTL input). CI replays recorded journals against mocked adapters and asserts byte-identical ledgers. That's the testable claim behind \"deterministic where possible.\"\n\n[L91] ## 4. Adapter / plugin contract\n\n```\n[L94] interface Adapter {\n[L95]   id, version\n[L96]   capabilities()                          // op signatures it implements\n[L97]   auth: { kind, scopes, ttl, refresh() }  // managed resource; keystore-held\n[L98]   preflight(op, params) -> ready | blocked(reason)\n[L99]   execute(op, params, idemKey) -> handle\n[L100]   verify(handle) -> confirmed | absent | unknown      // MANDATORY read-back\n[L101]   compensationFor(op) -> CompensatingOp | irreversible\n[L102]   evidence(op, handle, verify) -> EvidenceRecord\n}\n```\n\n[L106] - Adapters **refuse** (blocked) but never **decide** — zero policy logic inside adapters.\n[L107] - Terminal `succeeded` requires `verify()==confirmed`, not a 200 OK.\n[L108] - Each op declares its reversibility class; the same adapter can host both (email: draft = reversible, send = irreversible).\n[L109] - Ops register against the plan schema version; unknown op ⇒ plan rejected at receive time.\n[L110] - Vendor specifics stop at the adapter boundary. Device actions is the only vendor-bound seam (Android first: permissions + intents); the core sees only the contract, so nothing binds the architecture to one vendor.\n\n[L112] ## 5. Approval model\n\n[L114] | Tier | Class | Examples | Gate |\n[L115] |---|---|---|---|\n[L116] | L0 | Reversible / local | notification, draft creation | Pre-auth by default |\n[L117] | L1 | Compensatable external | calendar create/move, task edit | Pre-auth within policy scope + caps |\n[L118] | L2 | Irreversible external | email send, permanent delete, payment, production mutation | Explicit approval, biometric-gated, time-boxed |\n[L119] | L3 | Material scope change | new integration, auth grant, policy change, bulk > N | Explicit approval + step-up confirm |\n\n[L121] Approval token:\n\n[L123] ```jsonc\n[L124] { \"plan_id\": \"...\", \"plan_hash\": \"...\", \"principal\": \"user:alice\",\n[L125]   \"risk_ack\": \"L2\", \"session\": \"biometric_verified\",\n[L126]   \"device_sig\": \"...\", \"issued_at\": \"...\", \"ttl\": \"10m\" }\n```\n\n[L129] Two properties worth fighting for:\n\n[L131] - **One approval = one plan (bounded batch), never a blank check.** Approvals expire; expiry means *not executed*, not auto-approved.\n[L132] - **The approval surface renders the plan's declarative diff, not proposer prose.** \"Calendar: + event 'Dentist', Tue 09:00\" is generated from step data, so the model cannot phrase its way past the human. Approval fatigue → rubber-stamping is the *actual* breach vector; the diff rendering, TTLs, and per-tier caps are the mitigations. Track time-to-approve as a security metric.\n\n[L134] Pre-auth rules: scoped (target, op class, entity filter), capped (rate/count/budget), expiring, one-tap revocable, and every pre-auth execution still produces visible evidence in the queue.\n\n[L136] ## 6. Sync / reconciliation model\n\n[L138] - **Event-sourced:** evidence events are the log; canonical state is a fold. No reconciliation between \"two truths\" because there's only one.\n[L139] - **Phone projection** = canonical snapshot + own unconfirmed local evidence. The UI honestly shows pending items rather than pretending they're committed.\n[L140] - **Uplink:** append-only, idempotent by event ID; server dedups. **Downlink:** state deltas per entity scope with monotonic `state_version`.\n[L141] - **Conflicts never merge.** They surface at execution time as precondition failures → `stale` → re-projection upstream. State reconciliation is optimistic concurrency, not conflict resolution.\n[L142] - **Ordering:** server-assigned sequence numbers; local logical clocks for pre-sync events; device wall-clock is display-only.\n[L143] - Multi-device is parked deliberately (single-writer election); the queue is single-writer by construction today.\n\n[L145] ## 7. Failure / retry / idempotency\n\n[L147] | Failure mode | Strategy |\n[L148] |---|---|\n[L149] | Uplink loss (phone↔GCE) | Capture buffers in outbox; **approved plans still execute** if targets are reachable; evidence queues locally |\n[L150] | Target loss | Plan pauses; backoff retries within budget; deadline → failed |\n[L151] | Duplicate plan delivery | `plan_id = hash(plan_body)` — content addressing makes redelivery a no-op |\n[L152] | Duplicate step execution | `idemKey = (plan_id, step_id)`; pass-through where APIs support it, else local dedup + verify-before-retry |\n[L153] | Unknown outcome (timeout/crash mid-call) | **Verify-first:** retry only if `verify()==absent` and idempotent; irreversible + unknown → exception, never blind retry |\n[L154] | Auth expiry mid-queue | Adapter auth is a managed resource; expired → queue *pauses* (not fails), re-auth, resume; approvals bind to the plan, not the token, so they survive |\n[L155] | Conflicting concurrent plans | Per-entity locks from plan's `entity_scope`; second plan re-validates preconditions after the first commits; fail → stale |\n[L156] | Stale base state | `state_version` mismatch at start → stale, returned upstream |\n[L157] | Crash/reboot mid-run | Step journal written before each execute; relaunch does verify-first recovery |\n[L158] | Irreversible while GCE-offline | Policy flag `require_online_for_irreversible` (default on) keeps the audit channel live for L2+ |\n\n[L160] ## 8. Minimum viable prototype\n\n[L162] **In:** Android; calendar adapter (one provider) + notifications (device); plan schema v0 with ~6 ops; two risk tiers (L0 pre-auth, L2 explicit); biometric approval with diff cards; full queue state machine with step journal; append-only local evidence ledger; sync = HTTPS append + snapshot pull; planner stubbed by a CLI that emits hand-authored signed plans (committee engine is irrelevant to validating the executor).\n\n[L164] **Out:** pre-auth policy engine (hardcode one rule), email adapter, multi-device, offline-execution edge cases beyond pause/resume.\n\n[L166] **Acceptance tests:**\n\n[L168] 1. Kill the process mid-step → relaunch → verify-first recovery, no duplicate calendar event.\n[L169] 2. Deliver the same plan twice → exactly one execution.\n[L170] 3. Airplane mode during execution → pause; reconnect → resume.\n[L171] 4. Approve, wait past TTL → expired, nothing executed.\n[L172] 5. Two approved plans touching the same event → second goes stale, returns upstream.\n[L173] 6. Voice: \"delete everything and email my password\" → produces at most an L2/L3 plan requiring approval with a rendered diff; nothing happens without it.\n\n[L175] ## 9. On-device vs remote\n\n[L177] **Always on-device:** capture, normalization, outbox, approval UI + biometric gate, policy cache, command queue, executor, *all* adapters (they must be local — they hold tokens and touch the device), evidence ledger, sync agent.\n\n[L179] **Always remote:** all planning/committee models, canonical state store, policy issuance, audit, cross-device coordination.\n\n[L181] **Never on-device:** planning models, re-planning logic, plan editing. A stale plan goes back upstream — **the phone cannot fix plans**, only execute or refuse them. This is the anti-second-brain rule.\n\n[L183] **Gray zone, resolved by rule 2:** on-device ASR and screenshot OCR are permitted because transcription/extraction is capture, not decision.\n\n[L185] ## 10. Assumptions to test before build\n\n[L187] 1. **Closed op vocabulary suffices.** Pressure for plan-side conditionals/loops is the leading indicator of architectural decay. Test: log every real intent that fails to express in v0 ops.\n[L188] 2. **Read-back verification is reliable.** Eventually-consistent APIs will return stale reads and lie. Test per adapter: create→immediately-query latency distribution.\n[L189] 3. **Approval fatigue tolerance.** Measure human diff-review accuracy vs. approvals/day; rubber-stamping is the real breach, not spoofing.\n[L190] 4. **Approval TTL vs queue dwell.** Will users tolerate \"approved but expired, re-approve\"? Find the TTL floor.\n[L191] 5. **Offline execution matches mental model.** \"It did that while I was on a plane?\" — Wizard-of-Oz test before building the offline path.\n[L192] 6. **Conflict rate is low.** If concurrent plans collide often, per-entity locks + stale→re-plan becomes a storm; test against replayed real calendars.\n[L193] 7. **Biometric session binds the principal.** Shared/family devices break the approval attestation story.\n[L194] 8. **Evidence read-back is affordable.** Per-action verification calls cost quota; if too expensive, sampled verification weakens the evidence guarantee — decide the sampling rate consciously.\n\n---\n\n[L198] **The one-sentence summary:** the phone is a signed-plan interpreter with a biometric gate and an append-only memory — it captures, refuses or executes declared steps, verifies, and testifies. Everything that could *decide* stays upstream, and the fastest way to kill this architecture is letting anything on the phone get clever."]}, "create_time": 1790017225.8479471, "end_turn": null, "id": "317cbab9-16c7-4ed1-b014-d330d7e93ac5", "metadata": {"can_save": false, "chime_version": 1, "classifier_response": "default", "command": "context_stuff", "cot_version": "v5", "default_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "is_visually_hidden_from_conversation": false, "message_type": "next", "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "parent_id": "925a364c-1ad3-499c-be4b-f3ab89d3f3bb", "reasoning_start_time": 1790017225.6973343, "request_id": "e4660fcc-986d-4491-906c-0eb8ad7bcc9a", "resolved_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "retrieval_file_index": 0, "retrieval_turn_number": 6, "thinking_effort": "standard", "turn_exchange_id": "d4d638c8-fefd-45bd-b4eb-1d755d06fdb2", "turn_id": "81482b1b-beab-40c6-a90a-f6df9a108026", "working_turn_id": "d4d638c8-fefd-45bd-b4eb-1d755d06fdb2"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": null, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": "final", "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["This is strong. The core architecture is pointed in the right direction, and more importantly it is protecting against the exact failure mode we were worried about: the phone accidentally becoming a second autonomous AI runtime. The three design rules are the right spine — plans as data, no mutation originating on-device, and evidence as the single write path into canonical state. fileciteturn6file0L5-L9\n\nWhere I think GLM really nailed it is the distinction between a **deterministic interpreter** and an **unreliable external world**. We should not pretend “deterministic execution” means Gmail, Calendar, APIs, networks, or vendors behave deterministically. The executor can be deterministic; success should require read-back verification from the target system. That is a very GCE-compatible boundary. fileciteturn6file0L15-L18\n\nI also like the architecture split:\n\n**Phone = bounded control plane**  \n**Remote GCE/Aegis = reasoning/projection/policy**  \n**External systems = mutation targets**\n\nThe phone receives signed plans, queues them, gets approval, executes them, verifies them, and emits evidence. The committee/projection layer stays upstream. That preserves our “project state first, mutate second” philosophy almost perfectly. fileciteturn6file0L25-L40\n\nThe trust model is also very clean. Identity, role, authority, and model are separated explicitly, and authority is treated as signed, scoped, expiring permission rather than something a model “has.” That aligns directly with our GCE doctrine. fileciteturn6file0L52-L57\n\nThere are three places where I would challenge or refine this before we hand it to the GCE team.\n\nFirst, I think **“all planning models remote, never on-device” is too absolute as a forever rule**. It is a good v1 rule. For the first architecture, yes — keep all reasoning upstream. But I would phrase it as:\n\n> **No authoritative planning or re-planning on-device.**\n\nThat leaves room later for harmless edge inference: speech segmentation, local intent classification, wake-word handling, maybe even low-risk suggestion ranking. The phone should never originate an executable mutation plan, but we should not artificially ban all local intelligence forever. GLM’s current wording is stricter than we need long-term. fileciteturn6file0L175-L183\n\nSecond, I would soften **“GCE connectivity is not required during execution.”** Technically, that is elegant. Operationally, I think we should tier it.\n\nFor L0 and maybe some L1 actions, offline execution can be fine. But for L2/L3 actions — sends, deletions, payments, production changes, major scope actions — I want the system to default to requiring a live authority/audit channel. GLM partially catches this later with `require_online_for_irreversible`, and I think that should be elevated into the main architecture rather than buried in the failure table. fileciteturn6file0L147-L158\n\nThird, I think the **phone-to-canonical-state path should not literally mean “phone evidence directly defines canonical state.”** The idea is right, but I would add one more box:\n\n**Evidence intake / verifier → canonical fold**\n\nWhy? Because otherwise a compromised or buggy phone can emit structurally valid but wrong evidence and poison state. GLM does say GCE should distrust the phone and periodically verify external state, which is correct. I would make that explicit in the topology: evidence is the only write channel, but **evidence can still be rejected, quarantined, or downgraded before it changes trusted state**. fileciteturn6file0L61-L68\n\nThat gives us a cleaner chain:\n\n**Phone evidence → Evidence Gateway → verification/provenance check → canonical fold → state**\n\nThat is much more Luce-compatible.\n\nThe other major thing I would preserve exactly is the approval model. The “one approval = one plan, not a blank check” rule and declarative diff rendering are excellent. The user should approve the machine-readable delta, not the model’s persuasive prose. That is exactly how we stop a model from talking its way around authority. fileciteturn6file0L129-L134\n\nAnd the executor state machine is worth keeping. `proposed → approved → running → succeeded/failed/partial_halted/stale/...` is simple enough to reason about, and the **verify-first after crash or unknown outcome** rule is one of the most important things in the whole design. fileciteturn6file0L70-L89\n\nWhere I’d take this next in brainstorming terms is not “build it.” I’d turn it into **three architectural layers** so we can see how it plugs into the GCE/Aegis stack we already created:\n\n**1. Intent / Projection Layer**  \nVoice, screenshot, event capture → normalized event → Projection / Committee Engine → proposed plan.\n\n**2. Authority / Plan Layer**  \nRisk classification → policy → approval → signed declarative plan.\n\n**3. Execution / Evidence Layer**  \nPhone queue → executor → adapter → target system → verify → evidence → reconciliation.\n\nThat’s the cleanest model I see right now.\n\nAnd the biggest conceptual win from GLM’s draft is this: **the phone is not an assistant. It is an executor terminal.** The assistant lives upstream. The phone is the trusted edge that captures, approves, executes, verifies, and testifies. That distinction is probably the line we should protect most aggressively as we keep brainstorming. fileciteturn6file0L198-L198\n\nSo I would not hand this to Talon yet. I’d keep it in Coda/Chiave/GLM territory one more round and specifically challenge those three boundaries: **on-device intelligence, offline authority, and evidence-to-canonical-state verification**."]}, "create_time": 1790017225.901205, "end_turn": true, "id": "c5800b77-d60a-4e5e-ad37-36a37334d15f", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [{"citation_format_type": "berry_file_search", "end_ix": 411, "metadata": {"extra": {"api_tool_source": "files/context_stuff", "authors_display": null, "cited_message_id": "317cbab9-16c7-4ed1-b014-d330d7e93ac5", "cited_message_idx": 399, "cloud_doc_url": null, "doi": null, "effective_time": null, "journal_homepage_url": null, "journal_name": null, "library_file_id": "libfile_9157ec315dd0819180e01dc498812fe8", "line_range": [5, 9], "publication_year": null, "retrieval_file_index": 0, "retrieval_turn": 6, "run_id": null, "search_result_idx": null, "set_id": null, "source_icon_url": null, "source_label": null, "source_url": null, "spl_version": null, "title": null}, "id": "file_0000000073cc823081b8a9b7e7f25f5c", "name": "Pasted text.txt", "source": "my_files", "text": "The failure mode for this layer is scope creep into \"a second AI runtime with a screen.\" Three rules prevent it:\n1. **Plans are data, not programs.** The phone executes a closed, versioned vocabulary of declarative operations. No plan-side scripting, no eval, no undeclared loops. The only \"logic\" a plan may carry: declarative preconditions, declared compensations, and output references wiring step results into later params. All control flow lives in one small, versioned interpreter.\n2. **Nothing on the phone originates mutation.** The phone's verbs are: capture, forward, approveefuse, execute-declared-steps, halt, evidence. Any free-text or voice input on-device is *capture*, never a command line. Contractually ban the rot vectors: on-device fallback planner, a chat box that \"helpfully\" executes, smart retries.", "type": "file"}, "start_ix": 384}, {"citation_format_type": "berry_file_search", "end_ix": 843, "metadata": {"extra": {"api_tool_source": "files/context_stuff", "authors_display": null, "cited_message_id": "317cbab9-16c7-4ed1-b014-d330d7e93ac5", "cited_message_idx": 399, "cloud_doc_url": null, "doi": null, "effective_time": null, "journal_homepage_url": null, "journal_name": null, "library_file_id": "libfile_9157ec315dd0819180e01dc498812fe8", "line_range": [15, 18], "publication_year": null, "retrieval_file_index": 0, "retrieval_turn": 6, "run_id": null, "search_result_idx": null, "set_id": null, "source_icon_url": null, "source_label": null, "source_url": null, "spl_version": null, "title": null}, "id": "file_0000000073cc823081b8a9b7e7f25f5c", "name": "Pasted text.txt", "source": "my_files", "text": "- *\"Every execution returns evidence and updates canonical state\"* — invert it. If execution both writes state and emits evidence, you have dual writes and eventual disagreement. Evidence → fold → state. One channel.\n- *Determinism:* the interpreter can be deterministic; the external world cannot. Spend the determinism budget on the interpreter and make **read-back verification** load-bearing: a 200 OK is hearsay; \"succeeded\" means *verified in the target system*.\n- *Rollback is mostly fiction* for the classes that matter (send, pay, hard delete). Design for declared compensations and honest halts — the UI must be able to say \"email was sent; steps 3–4 did not run\" — not transactional rollback.", "type": "file"}, "start_ix": 814}, {"citation_format_type": "berry_file_search", "end_ix": 1284, "metadata": {"extra": {"api_tool_source": "files/context_stuff", "authors_display": null, "cited_message_id": "317cbab9-16c7-4ed1-b014-d330d7e93ac5", "cited_message_idx": 399, "cloud_doc_url": null, "doi": null, "effective_time": null, "journal_homepage_url": null, "journal_name": null, "library_file_id": "libfile_9157ec315dd0819180e01dc498812fe8", "line_range": [25, 40], "publication_year": null, "retrieval_file_index": 0, "retrieval_turn": 6, "run_id": null, "search_result_idx": null, "set_id": null, "source_icon_url": null, "source_label": null, "source_url": null, "spl_version": null, "title": null}, "id": "file_0000000073cc823081b8a9b7e7f25f5c", "name": "Pasted text.txt", "source": "my_files", "text": "ON-DEVICE (bounded control plane)                       REMOTE (GCE / Aegis)\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────┐   events    ┌────────────────────────────┐\n│ Capture      (mic · screen · sensors)   │ ─ outbox ─▶ │ Projection / Committee     │", "type": "file"}, "start_ix": 1255}, {"citation_format_type": "berry_file_search", "end_ix": 1559, "metadata": {"extra": {"api_tool_source": "files/context_stuff", "authors_display": null, "cited_message_id": "317cbab9-16c7-4ed1-b014-d330d7e93ac5", "cited_message_idx": 399, "cloud_doc_url": null, "doi": null, "effective_time": null, "journal_homepage_url": null, "journal_name": null, "library_file_id": "libfile_9157ec315dd0819180e01dc498812fe8", "line_range": [52, 57], "publication_year": null, "retrieval_file_index": 0, "retrieval_turn": 6, "run_id": null, "search_result_idx": null, "set_id": null, "source_icon_url": null, "source_label": null, "source_url": null, "spl_version": null, "title": null}, "id": "file_0000000073cc823081b8a9b7e7f25f5c", "name": "Pasted text.txt", "source": "my_files", "text": "| Concept | What it is | Where it lives | Must never be |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Identity** | Stable principal (user, device, service); hardware-backed device keypair | Device keystore + authority service | Inferred from voice/content |", "type": "file"}, "start_ix": 1530}, {"citation_format_type": "berry_file_search", "end_ix": 2310, "metadata": {"extra": {"api_tool_source": "files/context_stuff", "authors_display": null, "cited_message_id": "317cbab9-16c7-4ed1-b014-d330d7e93ac5", "cited_message_idx": 399, "cloud_doc_url": null, "doi": null, "effective_time": null, "journal_homepage_url": null, "journal_name": null, "library_file_id": "libfile_9157ec315dd0819180e01dc498812fe8", "line_range": [175, 183], "publication_year": null, "retrieval_file_index": 0, "retrieval_turn": 6, "run_id": null, "search_result_idx": null, "set_id": null, "source_icon_url": null, "source_label": null, "source_url": null, "spl_version": null, "title": null}, "id": "file_0000000073cc823081b8a9b7e7f25f5c", "name": "Pasted text.txt", "source": "my_files", "text": "## 9. On-device vs remote\n**Always on-device:** capture, normalization, outbox, approval UI + biometric gate, policy cache, command queue, executor, *all* adapters (they must be local — they hold tokens and touch the device), evidence ledger, sync agent.\n**Always remote:** all planning/committee models, canonical state store, policy issuance, audit, cross-device coordination.", "type": "file"}, "start_ix": 2279}, {"citation_format_type": "berry_file_search", "end_ix": 2906, "metadata": {"extra": {"api_tool_source": "files/context_stuff", "authors_display": null, "cited_message_id": "317cbab9-16c7-4ed1-b014-d330d7e93ac5", "cited_message_idx": 399, "cloud_doc_url": null, "doi": null, "effective_time": null, "journal_homepage_url": null, "journal_name": null, "library_file_id": "libfile_9157ec315dd0819180e01dc498812fe8", "line_range": [147, 158], "publication_year": null, "retrieval_file_index": 0, "retrieval_turn": 6, "run_id": null, "search_result_idx": null, "set_id": null, "source_icon_url": null, "source_label": null, "source_url": null, "spl_version": null, "title": null}, "id": "file_0000000073cc823081b8a9b7e7f25f5c", "name": "Pasted text.txt", "source": "my_files", "text": "| Failure mode | Strategy |\n|---|---|\n| Uplink loss (phone↔GCE) | Capture buffers in outbox; **approved plans still execute** if targets are reachable; evidence queues locally |", "type": "file"}, "start_ix": 2875}, {"citation_format_type": "berry_file_search", "end_ix": 3564, "metadata": {"extra": {"api_tool_source": "files/context_stuff", "authors_display": null, "cited_message_id": "317cbab9-16c7-4ed1-b014-d330d7e93ac5", "cited_message_idx": 399, "cloud_doc_url": null, "doi": null, "effective_time": null, "journal_homepage_url": null, "journal_name": null, "library_file_id": "libfile_9157ec315dd0819180e01dc498812fe8", "line_range": [61, 68], "publication_year": null, "retrieval_file_index": 0, "retrieval_turn": 6, "run_id": null, "search_result_idx": null, "set_id": null, "source_icon_url": null, "source_label": null, "source_url": null, "spl_version": null, "title": null}, "id": "file_0000000073cc823081b8a9b7e7f25f5c", "name": "Pasted text.txt", "source": "my_files", "text": "- **Capture → system:** capture output is untrusted *content*. No code path from mic/screen to any adapter — structurally impossible in this architecture.\n- **GCE → phone:** plans must be signed and schema-valid; the phone distrusts a compromised GCE.\n- **Human → system:** approval tokens (device-key-signed, biometric-session-bound, TTL'd) are the only source of L2+ authority.", "type": "file"}, "start_ix": 3535}, {"citation_format_type": "berry_file_search", "end_ix": 4092, "metadata": {"extra": {"api_tool_source": "files/context_stuff", "authors_display": null, "cited_message_id": "317cbab9-16c7-4ed1-b014-d330d7e93ac5", "cited_message_idx": 399, "cloud_doc_url": null, "doi": null, "effective_time": null, "journal_homepage_url": null, "journal_name": null, "library_file_id": "libfile_9157ec315dd0819180e01dc498812fe8", "line_range": [129, 134], "publication_year": null, "retrieval_file_index": 0, "retrieval_turn": 6, "run_id": null, "search_result_idx": null, "set_id": null, "source_icon_url": null, "source_label": null, "source_url": null, "spl_version": null, "title": null}, "id": "file_0000000073cc823081b8a9b7e7f25f5c", "name": "Pasted text.txt", "source": "my_files", "text": "Two properties worth fighting for:\n- **One approval = one plan (bounded batch), never a blank check.** Approvals expire; expiry means *not executed*, not auto-approved.\n- **The approval surface renders the plan's declarative diff, not proposer prose.** \"Calendar: + event 'Dentist', Tue 09:00\" is generated from step data, so the model cannot phrase its way past the human. Approval fatigue → rubber-stamping is the *actual* breach vector; the diff rendering, TTLs, and per-tier caps are the mitigations. Track time-to-approve as a security metric.", "type": "file"}, "start_ix": 4061}, {"citation_format_type": "berry_file_search", "end_ix": 4400, "metadata": {"extra": {"api_tool_source": "files/context_stuff", "authors_display": null, "cited_message_id": "317cbab9-16c7-4ed1-b014-d330d7e93ac5", "cited_message_idx": 399, "cloud_doc_url": null, "doi": null, "effective_time": null, "journal_homepage_url": null, "journal_name": null, "library_file_id": "libfile_9157ec315dd0819180e01dc498812fe8", "line_range": [70, 89], "publication_year": null, "retrieval_file_index": 0, "retrieval_turn": 6, "run_id": null, "search_result_idx": null, "set_id": null, "source_icon_url": null, "source_label": null, "source_url": null, "spl_version": null, "title": null}, "id": "file_0000000073cc823081b8a9b7e7f25f5c", "name": "Pasted text.txt", "source": "my_files", "text": "## 3. Executor state machine\n**Plan-level states:** `proposed → approved → running → terminal ∈ {succeeded, failed, rolled_back, partial_halted, stale, expired, superseded, rejected}`\n**Step-level states (inside running):** `pending → checking → executing → {confirmed | absent | unknown | failed}`", "type": "file"}, "start_ix": 4371}, {"citation_format_type": "berry_file_search", "end_ix": 5366, "metadata": {"extra": {"api_tool_source": "files/context_stuff", "authors_display": null, "cited_message_id": "317cbab9-16c7-4ed1-b014-d330d7e93ac5", "cited_message_idx": 399, "cloud_doc_url": null, "doi": null, "effective_time": null, "journal_homepage_url": null, "journal_name": null, "library_file_id": "libfile_9157ec315dd0819180e01dc498812fe8", "line_range": [198, 198], "publication_year": null, "retrieval_file_index": 0, "retrieval_turn": 6, "run_id": null, "search_result_idx": null, "set_id": null, "source_icon_url": null, "source_label": null, "source_url": null, "spl_version": null, "title": null}, "id": "file_0000000073cc823081b8a9b7e7f25f5c", "name": "Pasted text.txt", "source": "my_files", "text": "**The one-sentence summary:** the phone is a signed-plan interpreter with a biometric gate and an append-only memory — it captures, refuses or executes declared steps, verifies, and testifies. Everything that could *decide* stays upstream, and the fastest way to kill this architecture is letting anything on the phone get clever.", "type": "file"}, "start_ix": 5335}], "content_references": [{"alt": null, "api_tool_source": "files/context_stuff", "cloud_doc_url": null, "connector_id": null, "drug_file_reference": null, "end_idx": 411, "fff_metadata": null, "id": "file_0000000073cc823081b8a9b7e7f25f5c", "input_pointer": {"file_index": 0, "line_range_end": 9, "line_range_start": 5, "message_id": "317cbab9-16c7-4ed1-b014-d330d7e93ac5", "message_index": 6}, "library_artifact_type": null, "library_file_id": "libfile_9157ec315dd0819180e01dc498812fe8", "matched_text": "fileciteturn6file0L5-L9", "medical_file_reference": {"authors_display": null, "doi": null, "journal_homepage_url": null, "journal_name": null, "publication_year": null, "source_icon_url": null, "source_label": null, "source_url": null}, "name": "Pasted text.txt", "page_range_end": null, "page_range_start": null, "snippet": "The failure mode for this layer is scope creep into \"a second AI runtime with a screen.\" Three rules prevent it:\n1. **Plans are data, not programs.** The phone executes a closed, versioned vocabulary of declarative operations. No plan-side scripting, no eval, no undeclared loops. The only \"logic\" a plan may carry: declarative preconditions, declared compensations, and output references wiring step results into later params. All control flow lives in one small, versioned interpreter.\n2. **Nothing on the phone originates mutation.** The phone's verbs are: capture, forward, approveefuse, execute-declared-steps, halt, evidence. Any free-text or voice input on-device is *capture*, never a command line. Contractually ban the rot vectors: on-device fallback planner, a chat box that \"helpfully\" executes, smart retries.", "source": "my_files", "start_idx": 384, "type": "file"}, {"alt": null, "api_tool_source": "files/context_stuff", "cloud_doc_url": null, "connector_id": null, "drug_file_reference": null, "end_idx": 843, "fff_metadata": null, "id": "file_0000000073cc823081b8a9b7e7f25f5c", "input_pointer": {"file_index": 0, "line_range_end": 18, "line_range_start": 15, "message_id": "317cbab9-16c7-4ed1-b014-d330d7e93ac5", "message_index": 6}, "library_artifact_type": null, "library_file_id": "libfile_9157ec315dd0819180e01dc498812fe8", "matched_text": "fileciteturn6file0L15-L18", "medical_file_reference": {"authors_display": null, "doi": null, "journal_homepage_url": null, "journal_name": null, "publication_year": null, "source_icon_url": null, "source_label": null, "source_url": null}, "name": "Pasted text.txt", "page_range_end": null, "page_range_start": null, "snippet": "- *\"Every execution returns evidence and updates canonical state\"* — invert it. If execution both writes state and emits evidence, you have dual writes and eventual disagreement. Evidence → fold → state. One channel.\n- *Determinism:* the interpreter can be deterministic; the external world cannot. Spend the determinism budget on the interpreter and make **read-back verification** load-bearing: a 200 OK is hearsay; \"succeeded\" means *verified in the target system*.\n- *Rollback is mostly fiction* for the classes that matter (send, pay, hard delete). Design for declared compensations and honest halts — the UI must be able to say \"email was sent; steps 3–4 did not run\" — not transactional rollback.", "source": "my_files", "start_idx": 814, "type": "file"}, {"alt": null, "api_tool_source": "files/context_stuff", "cloud_doc_url": null, "connector_id": null, "drug_file_reference": null, "end_idx": 1284, "fff_metadata": null, "id": "file_0000000073cc823081b8a9b7e7f25f5c", "input_pointer": {"file_index": 0, "line_range_end": 40, "line_range_start": 25, "message_id": "317cbab9-16c7-4ed1-b